     "result_text_tail": " and is the main second-wave move.\n- The draft claimed the plan could keep FY2026 above the covenant. That is replaced by the lender talks.\n\nAs a result the recommendation moved from \"falls short of 3.0%\" to \"3.0% is reachable, but it depends on vendors\".\n\n**Choices I made on your behalf:**\n- **Mode, audience and theme:** a full deck, for the CEO presenting to the board, in the default `navy` theme.\n- **Fiscal year:** I read FY2026 as the year to January 2027, so the plan runs FY2027\u2013FY2028.\n- **Store split:** the pack doesn't give figures store by store. I assumed the six stores whose leases end carry six-ninths of the nine weak stores' sales and losses.\n- **Shrink quartiles:** I assumed each staffing quartile has equal sales, which matches the chain's 2.6% average shrink.\n- **Vendor funding:** I read the 14-point promotional margin gap as being after vendor funding. That is our interpretation, and it is the most uncertain number in the plan.\n- **No sales growth:** costs and sales are held at FY2025 levels. If wages keep rising 4.7% a year with prices flat, each further year costs about $8.5M; this is stated on slide 20.\n\nPossible next steps: switch theme, add a backup page on the vendor-funding calculation (the most exposed number), or turn the second wave into its own one-page plans.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- `harlow-margin-plan.pptx`\n- `harlow-margin-plan-sources.xlsx` (one sheet per slide, plus live formulas and assumptions)\n- `harlow-margin-plan.json` (the spec)"
    }
